WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.061 --> 00:00:19.285
and Grandpa were reading quietly together on the couch, when Laurie suddenly jumped up.

2
00:00:19.305 --> 00:00:21.626
"I almost forgot!

3
00:00:22.587 --> 00:00:24.969
Our teacher gave us each a tulip bulb today.

4
00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:29.012
She said if we plant it now, it should bloom in the spring."

5
00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.621
Laurie reached into her backpack.

6
00:00:34.041 --> 00:00:42.485
"Perfect. I was just reading some poetry by an eighteenth-century Turkish poet," Grandpa said, smiling dreamily.

7
00:00:43.565 --> 00:00:45.166
Laurie stared at her grandpa.

8
00:00:45.186 --> 00:00:49.628
"I said I got a tulip bulb today."

9
00:00:50.949 --> 00:00:54.370
Grandpa turned to her with the dreamy smile still on his face.

10
00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:58.051
"Do you know where tulips come from?"

11
00:00:58.392 --> 00:00:58.932
he asked.

12
00:01:00.072 --> 00:01:02.293
"Holland?" "Nope.

13
00:01:02.674 --> 00:01:10.939
Turkey. And the word 'tulip' comes from the Turkish word for turban because tulip bulbs and buds look

14
00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:12.319
like turbans, don't they?"

15
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.602
Laurie smiled as she examined the bulb in her hand.

16
00:01:16.622 --> 00:01:18.363
"You're right!"

17
00:01:18.383 --> 00:01:22.866
"The period from 1718 to 1730 is known in Ottoman history as the Tulip Era.

18
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:34.602
It was a time of peace for the Turkish Ottoman Empire, when the arts, culture, and architecture flourished.

19
00:01:35.842 --> 00:01:41.424
And—your bulb reminded me of this—tulip mania swept over the empire.

20
00:01:42.684 --> 00:01:50.066
Everyone, including the ruler, Sultan Ahmed III, was absolutely obsessed with tulips."

21
00:01:50.086 --> 00:01:53.227
"How funny," Laurie said.

22
00:01:53.247 --> 00:01:56.147
"So is a sultan like a king?"

23
00:01:56.167 --> 00:02:01.081
"Yes, a sultan is the ruler of a Muslim empire.

24
00:02:02.202 --> 00:02:11.106
The Ottoman Empire began in 1300 when a Muslim prince named Osman claimed a small area of land

25
00:02:11.166 --> 00:02:12.127
in western Turkey.

26
00:02:13.307 --> 00:02:19.550
Osman fought to expand his territory, and after he died, his son continued the expansion.

27
00:02:21.411 --> 00:02:28.774
Many years and many wars later, the Ottoman Empire had conquered neighboring Muslim dynasties in the Middle East,

28
00:02:29.254 --> 00:02:31.835
and most of the Eastern Roman Empire too.

29
00:02:33.015 --> 00:02:38.777
By the seventeenth century, the Ottomans controlled parts of Europe, Africa, and Asia.

30
00:02:39.797 --> 00:02:45.279
And the empire was always ruled by a sultan who was a male descendant of Osman.

31
00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:48.780
Sultans had luxurious lives.

32
00:02:49.996 --> 00:02:55.822
They lived in the best houses, wore the finest clothes, and ate the most delicious foods in the

33
00:02:55.902 --> 00:03:00.586
empire. They were also extremely sheltered from their people.

34
00:03:01.787 --> 00:03:09.755
In the 1400s Sultan Mehmed II conquered the city of Istanbul, made it the capital, and ordered a

35
00:03:09.855 --> 00:03:11.517
huge palace to be built there.

36
00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:15.804
The palace was called Topkapi.

37
00:03:15.824 --> 00:03:21.167
It was like a mini city, with multiple buildings, courtyards, mosques, and towers.

38
00:03:22.488 --> 00:03:29.051
This huge palace kept the sultan almost completely separated from his officials, soldiers, and subjects.

39
00:03:30.212 --> 00:03:38.897
But things change from generation to generation, and in the 1700s Sultan Ahmed III didn't like feeling locked

40
00:03:38.977 --> 00:03:41.098
up in stuffy, old Topkapi.

41
00:03:42.218 --> 00:03:44.800
He loved to celebrate and have parties.

42
00:03:45.576 --> 00:03:48.057
And he wanted to do this outside the palace.

43
00:03:49.237 --> 00:03:53.138
His grand vizier at the time—" "What's a grand vizier?"

44
00:03:53.518 --> 00:03:54.459
Laurie interrupted.

45
00:03:54.479 --> 00:04:00.960
"The grand vizier was the highest official in the Ottoman Empire after the sultan.

46
00:04:02.061 --> 00:04:06.422
He represented the sultan and was also the ruler's main adviser.

47
00:04:07.602 --> 00:04:13.064
The grand vizier was so important that only the sultan could hire or fire him.

48
00:04:14.208 --> 00:04:21.850
So the grand vizier suggested that the sultan build new royal palaces, along with mosques and public fountains

49
00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:23.711
that could be enjoyed by everyone.

50
00:04:25.111 --> 00:04:29.412
Not only would this make the sultan happy, but it would make him popular!

51
00:04:30.593 --> 00:04:33.654
You've heard of the French Palace of Versailles, haven't you?"

52
00:04:33.674 --> 00:04:38.215
"Yep. Marie Antoinette lived there!"

53
00:04:38.555 --> 00:04:41.116
Laurie said, proud to know something important.

54
00:04:42.308 --> 00:04:47.552
"Apparently Sultan Ahmed III was fascinated by the culture of Versailles.

55
00:04:48.693 --> 00:04:57.158
He had heard of the fancy parties and great banquets He wanted to bring a bit of that

56
00:04:57.459 --> 00:04:58.779
to the Ottoman Empire.

57
00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.463
So he built fountains and mosques that could be lit up at night.

58
00:05:04.603 --> 00:05:07.345
And he built a new palace with beautiful gardens.

59
00:05:08.486 --> 00:05:15.746
The gardens were of course filled with tulips— tulips with round petals, tulips with pointed petals, tulips with

60
00:05:15.886 --> 00:05:19.527
brilliant yellow petals, and tulips streaked red and purple.

61
00:05:20.808 --> 00:05:27.771
Acrobats and entertainers performed in these gardens, while artists sketched, and poets wrote beneath the stars.

62
00:05:29.111 --> 00:05:37.855
And sometimes, on really special nights, the royal gardeners strapped candles onto the backs of turtles and released

63
00:05:37.915 --> 00:05:39.876
them in between the rows of tulips.

64
00:05:41.036 --> 00:05:44.319
The colorful flowers were illuminated all night."

65
00:05:44.339 --> 00:05:47.782
"That sounds like fun," Laurie said.

66
00:05:47.802 --> 00:05:49.843
"Were kids allowed to go?"

67
00:05:49.863 --> 00:05:53.306
"I'm sure the royal children were.

68
00:05:54.527 --> 00:05:56.008
At least until their bedtime.

69
00:05:57.209 --> 00:06:01.072
Sounds like a happy time to be living in the Ottoman Empire, doesn't it?"

70
00:06:01.393 --> 00:06:02.233
Grandpa asked.

71
00:06:02.253 --> 00:06:05.476
"Only if you're invited to the parties!"

72
00:06:05.916 --> 00:06:06.637
Laurie said.

73
00:06:06.657 --> 00:06:10.140
"Clever girl," Grandpa said, nodding.

74
00:06:11.328 --> 00:06:14.410
"Naturally the entire empire was not invited.

75
00:06:15.471 --> 00:06:22.095
And even though it was one of the wealthiest periods in Ottoman history, not everyone enjoyed such luxury.

76
00:06:23.335 --> 00:06:26.978
Peasants who were struggling to earn a living each day were unhappy.

77
00:06:27.918 --> 00:06:29.879
And so were the janissaries."

78
00:06:29.899 --> 00:06:32.361
"Who were the janissaries?"

79
00:06:32.741 --> 00:06:33.461
Laurie asked.

80
00:06:33.481 --> 00:06:37.204
"They were the Ottoman Empire's soldiers.

81
00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:42.504
During the empire's periods of conquest, they were quite respected.

82
00:06:43.625 --> 00:06:46.327
But in peace time, they didn't have anything to do!

83
00:06:47.848 --> 00:06:52.032
Some became gardeners or craftsmen, and others became restless.

84
00:06:53.193 --> 00:06:58.858
Eventually war broke out in the 1720s on the empire's borders with Iran.

85
00:07:00.199 --> 00:07:06.084
Ottoman troops went to Iran, but they failed to settle anything, and the Ottomans lost some territory.

86
00:07:07.264 --> 00:07:12.568
Unhappy with the sultan's weak military response, a group of janissaries revolted.

87
00:07:13.709 --> 00:07:18.072
The sultan was forced to resign and had the grand vizier executed.

88
00:07:19.293 --> 00:07:25.678
For a few weeks, there was chaos as the rebels burned palaces and destroyed signs of excess wealth.

89
00:07:26.959 --> 00:07:33.384
Finally the new sultan, Mahmud, put an end to the rebellion, but the happy times were over.

90
00:07:34.568 --> 00:07:40.690
Mahmud was occupied with wars against Iran and Russia for the rest of his reign, and didn't have

91
00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:43.271
much time for fancy garden parties."

92
00:07:43.291 --> 00:07:47.692
"No more tulips for the Ottoman Empire?"

93
00:07:48.052 --> 00:07:50.553
Laurie asked, looking down at the bulb in her palm.

94
00:07:50.573 --> 00:07:56.695
"Well, tulips are still a pride of Turkey and appear in lots of Turkish art.

95
00:07:57.795 --> 00:08:01.376
We could call them a legacy of Sultan Ahmed III's reign."

96
00:08:02.612 --> 00:08:04.677
Laurie put the bulb on top of her head.

97
00:08:04.697 --> 00:08:08.867
"I'm going to go plant my turban now," she said, giggling.

98
00:08:08.887 --> 00:08:12.155
"See you later, Grandpa."
